(106, 'mosesabuya812@gmail.com', 'Moses', 'Olouch', '', 'magazine', '41.209.57.142', 'assets/uploads/678e38708b8aa-yoga-practice-mountains.jpg,assets/uploads/678e38708e2c1-back5.webp,assets/uploads/678e38708e96f-b2.webp,assets/uploads/678e38708f5fb-b1.webp', '', '13:50:08', '2025-01-20', 0),
(107, 'gogajohn22@gmail.com', 'john', 'goga', 'A small town lay by a river that once flowed with clear, sparkling water. Over time pollution tainted it and the villagers ignored the signs. One day a young boy named John stood at the river&#39;s edge noticing its struggle. He told his parents. &#34;The river is sick. We must help it heal.&#34; The villagers moved by his words began cleaning the river and reducing waste. Slowly under the water cleared and life returned. John&#39;s simple plea reminded everyone that caring for nature was essential for their future. ', 'advertise', '102.0.7.130', '', '', '22:39:19', '2025-02-01', 0),
(108, '', '', '', '', NULL, '102.0.7.130', '', '', '22:39:29', '2025-02-01', 0),
(109, '', '', '', '', NULL, '197.232.135.49', 'assets/uploads/67ab1e86045e6-cartoon for turning point  publication-03.jpg,assets/uploads/67ab1e860598c-cartoon for turning point  publication-04.jpg', '', '11:55:18', '2025-02-11', 0),
(110, 'bayahsamson01@gmail.com', 'Bayah', 'Samson', 'Bayah Samson â€“ Documentary & Street Photojournalist\r\nMy journey into photography began not with a camera, but with curiosity, a deep need to observe, to understand, and to preserve moments that often pass unnoticed. Iâ€™ve always been drawn to people and their stories: the wrinkled face of a street vendor, children playing barefoot in dusty alleyways, a motherâ€™s silent prayer as she watched her child. These were the images I wanted to hold onto, long before I knew how to frame them through a lens.\r\nIn the early days, I used whatever camera I could get my hands onâ€”sometimes a borrowed smartphone, other times an old point-and-shoot. I didnâ€™t start with formal training; I started with instinct. I learned through trial and error, by closely watching the world and chasing light wherever it led me. Over time, I taught myself the technical aspects of photography, but more importantly, I learned to listen to people, to spaces, to silences.\r\nWhat drew me deeper into photography was realizing that images could do more than document, they could speak, challenge, inform, and inspire. That understanding guided me toward photojournalism. I no longer wanted to simply take beautiful pictures; I wanted to tell meaningful stories. Stories that matter. Stories that might otherwise go unnoticed.\r\nBased in Mombasa, I began photographing everyday life with a documentary eye, focusing on untold narratives of struggle, resilience, and humanity within my community. My work naturally evolved into photojournalism, where the camera became more than a creative tool, it became a witness and a voice.\r\nToday, as a young photojournalist, I aim to capture the emotional truth of real people and real moments. My photography is a form of storytelling that I hope creates awareness, sparks conversations, and contributes in some way to social change. The journey is ongoing, but every frame I shoot is a reminder of why I began and why I canâ€™t stop.', 'magazine', '196.96.233.237', 'assets/uploads/67f9a0841c5ea-inbound5289676022618910304.jpg', '&#34;More Than a Vendor: The Silent Power of Kisiiâ€™s Community Ambassador&#34;\r\nOn a chilly afternoon of March 6, 2025, in the heart of Kisii Town, I captured a woman whose presence transcended the ordinary rhythm of the street. Draped in humble attire and balancing baskets of fresh fruits with practiced grace, she moved with quiet determination. What first caught my eye wasnâ€™t just her hustleâ€”it was her shirt, boldly tagged Community Ambassador.\r\nThat simple tag said everything. It wasnâ€™t just a title; it was a testament. It spoke of recognition, of purpose, of someone who had earned respect not through grand speeches but through consistent action, service, and unwavering presence in the lives of those around her. She wasnâ€™t merely selling fruit, she was feeding a neighborhood, offering warmth in conversation, and standing as a symbol of hope and resilience.\r\nMore than a hustler, she is a leader, a pillar, a true Community Ambassador. Her work echoes far beyond the market stalls. In that fleeting moment behind my lens, I didnâ€™t just see a vendor, I saw a woman shaping her world, one fruit, one smile, and one silent act of leadership at a time.', '01:06:44', '2025-04-12', 0),
(111, '', '', '', '', NULL, '196.96.233.237', '', '', '01:07:01', '2025-04-12', 0),
(112, '', '', '', '', NULL, '196.96.233.237', '', '', '01:07:25', '2025-04-12', 0),
